---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import DocsKit from '../../components/picks-docs/DocsKit.astro';
import { STUDIO_PLUGIN_INSTALL_HREF, STUDIO_PLUGIN_STORE_LIVE } from '../../lib/studio-plugin';
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Connect a project — Apple Docs"
  description="Pair the Apple Studio plugin with one Apple project using a short-lived code, and understand what the connection covers and how long it lasts."
  heading="Connect a project"
>
  {/* Availability comes BEFORE the steps, asked of the shared flag, so nobody follows five steps
      for a plugin they cannot get (apps/site/tests/onboarding-recovery.test.mjs). */}
  {STUDIO_PLUGIN_STORE_LIVE ? (
    <div class="callout">
      <p><strong>No plugin yet?</strong> Apple Studio is free on the Roblox Creator Store. <a href={STUDIO_PLUGIN_INSTALL_HREF} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Get Apple Studio</a>, then follow <a href="/docs/plugin">Install the plugin</a> and come back here.</p>
    </div>
  ) : (
    <div class="callout">
      <p><strong>Public Studio installation is unavailable right now.</strong> These steps are for people who already have a working Apple plugin. New customers can use chat, but cannot build inside Studio yet. <a href="/docs/plugin">Plugin availability</a>.</p>
    </div>
  )}
  <p>
    Connecting links the Apple panel in <em>this</em> Studio window to <em>one</em> Apple project.
    Until you do it, the plugin is inert: the panel reads <strong>Not connected</strong> and Apple
    cannot see or change anything.
  </p>
  <p>
    The link uses a short-lived pairing code instead of your password. Treat the code as a
    temporary credential: enter it only in your own Apple plugin and do not share it.
  </p>

  <h2>Connecting</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the place you want Apple to work on in Roblox Studio.</li>
    <li>
      In Studio's <strong>Plugins</strong> tab, click <strong>Apple</strong>. The
      <strong>Apple Studio</strong> panel docks and shows <strong>Not connected</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>
      In the Apple web workspace, open the project. Until Studio is linked, the conversation ends
      with a <strong>Connect Roblox Studio</strong> block — press <strong>Enter pairing code</strong>
      on its last step. (The <strong>Connect</strong> button in the project's <strong>Studio</strong>
      panel opens the same thing.) You get a six-character code — something like <code>K7M3QP</code>
      — with a countdown.
    </li>
    <li>
      Type that code into the plugin panel and press <strong>Connect to Apple</strong>.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The panel reads <strong>Connected</strong> followed by the project name, and the button becomes
    <strong>Disconnect</strong>. The web workspace stops saying it is waiting for Studio and shows the
    place you have open. The access line reads <strong>Access: inspect only</strong>: Apple can now
    read this place, and it cannot change anything yet.
  </p>

  <h2>Allowing edits</h2>
  <p>
    Edits are allowed per connection, and every new connection starts with them off. In the panel,
    press <strong>Enable edits…</strong> and confirm with <strong>Allow edits for this
    connection</strong>. The access line changes to <strong>Access: edits allowed for this
    connection</strong>, and Apple can build when this project's chat asks it to. Press
    <strong>Turn edits off</strong> at any time to go back to inspect only.
  </p>

  <h2>About the code</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>It expires after 10 minutes</strong> and works once. Expired or already used, it is dead — generate a new one.</li>
    <li>
      It is six characters drawn from an alphabet with no confusable letters — no O, I, L or 1 —
      so there is nothing to squint at.
    </li>
    <li>
      Case does not matter and punctuation is ignored, so <code>k7m3qp</code> and
      <code>K7M-3QP</code> both work.
    </li>
    <li>You can have up to five unused codes alive at once, across all your projects.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What the connection covers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>One Studio window, one project.</strong> Opening a different place, or a second
      Studio window, does not carry the connection with it — connect that window too.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It survives restarts.</strong> The plugin remembers the session, so reopening Studio
      reconnects without a new code.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It expires after 30 days.</strong> When it does, the panel says
      <em>Session ended — reconnect with a new code</em>. Generate a fresh code and connect again.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Disconnecting</h2>
  <p>
    Press <strong>Disconnect</strong> in the plugin panel, or disconnect from the web workspace.
    Either one stops Apple acting on the place immediately, whether or not the plugin stays
    installed. Nothing Apple already built is undone — use Studio's undo or a checkpoint for that.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Team Create:</strong> the connection belongs to your Studio window, and Apple's edits
      reach your teammates the same way your own edits do. Teammates who want their own Apple chat
      should connect their own Studio window to their own project.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Something not lining up? <a href="/docs/troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a> lists the exact
    messages the panel can show and what each one means.
  </p>

  <DocsKit />
</DocsLayout>
